[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch0
diagnosis_text:
Router0 is not down. It is healthy and reachable on other interfaces: from cs_lab1, ping to 192.168.1.1 succeeds with 0% loss, and router0 shows eth0 up with 192.168.1.1/24 configured and link detected. The outage is confined to the IT Department’s L2 path.

Mechanism: IT hosts cannot ARP their default gateway. On it_lab1, the ARP entry for 192.168.1.1 shows “FAILED” and pings return “Destination Host Unreachable,” indicating no L2 reachability. Likewise, router0 cannot reach it_lab1 across the IT segment and returns “Destination Host Unreachable.”

Localization: switch0 (the L2 bridge connecting router0.eth0 to lan_it) is not forwarding. On switch0, brctl/bridge output shows br0 exists with ports eth0 and eth1, but the bridge is DOWN and both member ports are in “state disabled.” ip -br addr confirms br0 is DOWN while eth0/eth1 are UP. With the bridge interface down, no frames pass between router0.eth0 (link_r0_sw0) and the IT PCs (lan_it).

Root cause: The Linux bridge br0 on switch0 is administratively down (or misconfigured), disabling forwarding on both ports and isolating the IT VLAN.

Corrective fix: On switch0, bring the bridge up and ensure ports are forwarding: ip link set br0 up; verify bridge link shows state forwarding on eth0 and eth1; keep STP enabled if desired. Alternatively, restart networking to restore the bridge. Afterward, ARP should resolve and IT hosts will regain access to gateway 192.168.1.1 and off-subnet routes.
=== END ===